"Towards better health conversations: Research insights on a “wayfinding” AI agent based on Gemini (https://research.google/blog/towards-better-health-conversations-research-insights-on-a-wayfinding-ai-agent-based-on-gemini/)\nciteturn3177view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://research.google/blog/towards-better-health-conversations-research-insights-on-a-wayfinding-ai-agent-based-on-gemini/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 172\nL0: cite0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com L1: ## Explore our many areas of focus\nL2: \nL3: cite1†Explore all research areas L4: \nL5: Applied AI & sciences\nL6: \nL7: cite2†Earth AI cite3†Health AI cite4†Science AI cite5†Sustainability & crisis resilience L8: \nL9: Foundational ML & algorithms\nL10: \nL11: cite6†Algorithms & theory cite7†Information retrieval cite8†Machine intelligence cite9†Machine perception cite10†Natural language processing L12: \nL13: People, systems & quantum AI\nL14: cite11†Human-computer interaction and visualization cite12†Networking cite13†Quantum AI cite14†Responsible AI cite15†Anti abuse cite16†Software engineering cite17†Software systems L15: \nL16: Learn More\nL17: \nL18: cite18†Publications cite19†Projects L19: ## Building a collaborative ecosystem\nL20: \nL21: cite20†Datasets Access high-quality datasets to accelerate your research. cite21†Tools & services Explore our latest AI models and products. L22: \nL23: cite22†Open source Discover open-source code and collaborate with the community. L24: ## Shaping the future together\nL25: \nL26: cite23†See all programs L27: \nL28: cite24†Faculty programs Participating in the academic research community through meaningful engagement with university faculty. cite25†Student programs Supporting the next generation of researchers through a wide range of programming. L29: \nL30: cite26†R&D programs Collaborating with industry and academic experts to support foundational science and innovation. cite27†Locations Find your place in our global offices and research labs. L31: ## Translating discovery into real-world impact\nL32: \nL33: cite28†People Our researchers drive advancements in computer science through both fundamental and applied research. L34: \nL35: cite29†Teams Collaborative groups tackling the world's most challenging AI problems. L36: \nL37: Research\nL38: ## Explore our many areas of focus\nL39: \nL40: cite1†Explore all research areas L41: \nL42: Applied AI & sciences\nL43: \nL44: cite2†Earth AI cite3†Health AI cite4†Science AI cite5†Sustainability & crisis resilience L45: \nL46: Foundational ML & algorithms\nL47: \nL48: cite6†Algorithms & theory cite7†Information retrieval cite8†Machine intelligence cite9†Machine perception cite10†Natural language processing L49: \nL50: People, systems & quantum AI\nL51: cite11†Human-computer interaction and visualization cite12†Networking cite13†Quantum AI cite14†Responsible AI cite15†Anti abuse cite16†Software engineering cite17†Software systems L52: \nL53: Learn More\nL54: \nL55: cite18†Publications cite19†Projects L56: \nL57: Resources\nL58: ## Building a collaborative ecosystem\nL59: \nL60: cite20†Datasets Access high-quality datasets to accelerate your research. cite21†Tools & services Explore our latest AI models and products. cite22†Open source Discover open-source code and collaborate with the community. L61: \nL62: cite30†Conferences & events L63: \nL64: Careers\nL65: ## Shaping the future together\nL66: \nL67: cite23†See all programs L68: \nL69: cite24†Faculty programs Participating in the academic research community through meaningful engagement with university faculty. cite25†Student programs Supporting the next generation of researchers through a wide range of programming. cite26†R&D programs Collaborating with industry and academic experts to support foundational science and innovation. cite27†Locations Find your place in our global offices and research labs. L70: \nL71: cite31†Blog L72: \nL73: About\nL74: ## Translating discovery into real-world impact\nL75: \nL76: cite28†People Our researchers drive advancements in computer science through both fundamental and applied research. cite29†Teams Collaborative groups tackling the world's most challenging AI problems. L77: \nL78: cite32†Google Research L79: \nL80: cite33†Google AI Learn about all our AI†ai.google cite34†Google DeepMind Explore the frontier of AI†deepmind.google cite35†Google Labs Try our AI experiments†labs.google L81: \nL82: [Button: Research ]\nL83: \nL84: [Button: Resources ]\nL85: \nL86: cite30†Conferences & events L87: [Button: Careers ]\nL88: \nL89: cite31†Blog L90: \nL91: [Button: About ]\nL92: \nL93: [Input: Search]\nL94: \nL95: Search\nL96: \nL97: cite36†Image: Towards better health conversations: Research insights on a “wayfinding” AI agent based on Gemini†storage.googleapis.com L98: # Towards better health conversations: Research insights on a “wayfinding” AI agent based on Gemini\nL99: \nL100: September 25, 2025\nL101: \nL102: Mike Schaekermann, Research Scientist, and Rory Sayres, Researcher, Google Research\nL103: \nL104: We share user insights from a novel research AI agent that helps people find their way to better health information through proactive conversational guidance, goal understanding, and tailored conversations.\nL105: The ability to find clear, relevant, and personalized health information is a cornerstone of empowerment for medical patients. Yet, navigating the world of online health information is often a confusing, overwhelming, and impersonal experience. We are met with a flood of generic information that does not account for our unique context, and it can be difficult to know what details are relevant.\nL106: Large language models (LLMs) have the potential to make this information more accessible and tailored. However, many AI tools today act as passive \"question-answerers\" — they provide a single, comprehensive answer to an initial query. But this isn't how an expert, like a doctor, helps someone navigate a complex topic. A health professional doesn't just provide a lecture; they ask clarifying questions to understand the full picture, discover a person's goals, and guide them through the information maze.\nL107: Though this context-seeking is critical, it's a significant design challenge for AI.\nL108: In “cite37†Towards Better Health Conversations: The Benefits of Context-Seeking†storage.googleapis.com ”, we describe how we designed and tested our “Wayfinding AI”, an early-stage research prototype, based on Gemini, that explores a new approach. Our fundamental thesis is that by proactively asking clarifying questions, an AI agent can better discover a user's needs, guide them in articulating their concerns, and provide more helpful, tailored information.\nL109: In a series of four mixed-method user experience studies with a total of 163 participants, we examined how people interact with AI for their health questions, and we iteratively designed an agent that users found to be significantly more helpful, relevant, and tailored to their needs than a baseline AI agent.\nL110: ## Formative user experience insights: Challenges in finding health information online\nL111: To better understand the hurdles people face, we interviewed 33 participants about their experiences finding health information online. A key theme quickly emerged: people often struggle to articulate their health concerns. As one participant described, their process was to \"...just kind of like throw all the words in there and then I'm just gonna see what comes back.\" It may be that without a clinical background, it’s difficult to know which details are medically relevant.\nL112: The people we interviewed were then able to use research prototypes of different chatbots. (The chat histories were not logged.) These participants made up a diverse group and asked health questions on a wide range of topics (e.g., rib pain, vertigo, consistent and unexplained weight gain, tinnitus and surgery; more details cite37†in the paper†storage.googleapis.com ). Our studies revealed that when a chatbot proactively asks clarifying questions, the experience changes dramatically.\nL113: The majority of participants preferred a \"deferred-answer\" approach — where the AI asks questions first — over one that gives a comprehensive answer immediately. This conversational style was perceived as more personal and reassuring. As one person noted, \"It feels more like the way it would work if you talk to a doctor...\nL114: it does make me feel a little more confident that it wants to know more before jumping right into an answer.\" These clarifying questions not only help the AI provide better answers, but also empower users, guiding them to provide more relevant context. We found similar patterns in cite38†prior work on AI for dermatology†dl.acm.org .\nL115: However, the effectiveness of this clarifying question–based approach depends heavily on the execution — engagement drops if questions are poorly formulated, irrelevant, or buried within long paragraphs of text where they are easily missed.\nL116: ## Designing a Wayfinding AI to empower people through personal and proactive conversations\nL117: \nL118: Informed by these insights, we designed our Wayfinding AI around three core principles to create a more empowering conversational experience:\nL119:   1. Proactive conversational guidance: At each turn, the Wayfinding AI asks up to three targeted questions designed to systematically reduce ambiguity. This helps users articulate their health story more completely and directly incorporates users’ desire for more contextualized answers.\nL120:   2. Best-effort answers at each turn: Because some health-related questions may not require clarification to get a good answer, the Wayfinding AI provides a \"best-effort\" answer at every conversational turn, based on the information shared so far, while emphasizing that the answer can be improved if the user can answer one or more of the follow-up questions.\nL121: This approach gives the user helpful information throughout the conversation, while providing the option to further receive increasingly better answers as the conversation progresses.\nL122:   3. Transparent reasoning: The Wayfinding AI explains how the user's latest answers have helped refine the previous answer. This makes the AI's reasoning process clear and understandable.\nL123: To ensure clarifying questions are never missed within the longer answers in the “best-effort” answers section, we designed an interface with a two-column layout. The conversation and clarifying questions appear in the left column, while best-effort answers and more detailed explanations appear in the right. This separates the interactive conversation from the informational content.\nL124: \nL125: play silent looping video pause silent looping video\nL126: \nL127: unmute video mute video\nL128: Example of a user starting to interact with our Wayfinding AI prototype interface, including both the familiar multi-turn chat interface on the left, and a “best information so far” panel on the right. This two-panel interface separates the context-seeking stream from the more detailed information provision piece, enabling users to dive into the information only when they feel all relevant information has been relayed.\nL129: ## Evaluating our Wayfinding AI through a randomized user study\nL130: To evaluate the potential real-world impact of this agent, we conducted a randomized user study with 130 US-based participants recruited via a third party platform. All participants were 21 years and older, were not health care professionals, and had a health-related question for which they were willing to interact with an AI. To ensure a broad range of health topics, we imposed very few restrictions on which topic would be eligible for the study (details on excluded inquiries are provided in the paper).\nL131: In a randomized cite39†within-subjects design†www.nngroup.com , each participant interacted with both our Wayfinding AI and a baseline Gemini 2.5 Flash model to explore their health topic. After providing informed consent and answering standard demographic questions, participants were instructed to have a conversation spending at least 3 minutes on their question; and then to resume the survey.\nL132: After interacting with each AI, participants answered questions about their satisfaction with the experience along 6 dimensions: helpfulness, relevance of questions asked, tailoring to their situation, goal understanding, ease of use, and efficiency of getting useful information. They were able to provide open feedback about what they learned, and also had the option to upload their conversation with the AI. Sharing the conversation was not required to complete the survey.\nL133: At the end of the study, participants were prompted to explicitly compare the two AIs and indicate which they would prefer in terms of each of the six dimensions above. They were also asked, \"For a future topic, would you prefer the first or the second AI?\" The order of AI exposure (Baseline AI first vs. Wayfinding AI first) was randomized across participants. Throughout the study, participants were instructed to not provide any identifying information about themselves.\nL134: cite40†Image: Wayfinder2_StudyDesign†storage.googleapis.com L135: \nL136: Illustration of our study design.\nL137: ## Helpful and relevant information through goal understanding and tailored conversations\nL138: As shown below, the results of the study demonstrated that users preferred the Wayfinding AI's approach across several important dimensions, despite its less-familiar two-column interface. Users favored Wayfinding AI for its helpfulness, relevance, ability to understand their goal, and for tailoring the conversation to their specific needs.\nL139: These findings suggest that the proactive, question-asking behavior of Wayfinding AI successfully created a more personalized and helpful experience for users without introducing undue friction in the user experience.\nL140: cite41†Image: Wayfinder3_Results†storage.googleapis.com L141: \nL142: User preferences between a baseline and our Wayfinding AI along multiple evaluation axes, including helpfulness of the agent, relevance of its responses, tailoring of the conversation to the user, understanding the user’s goal, ease of use, efficiency of the conversation and willingness to use each for a future health information need.\nL143: Beyond simply preferring their conversations with the Wayfinding AI, participants had noticeably different conversations. Conversations were longer with the Wayfinding AI, in particular when participants were trying to understand the cause of their symptoms. For those topics, conversations with the Wayfinding AI had 4.96 turns on average, compared to 3.29 for the baseline AI. And the pattern of prompts they provided to each AI looked different across conversations:\nL144: cite42†Image: Wayfinder4_HeroSankey†storage.googleapis.com L145: \nL146: Sankey diagram illustrating the flow of conversations with the baseline AI and the Wayfinding AI. Each of the vertical bars shows the breakdown of the types of user prompts, across the first 5 conversation turns. The blue bars indicate participants responding to clarifying questions — much more common for the Wayfinding AI.\nL147: ## Conclusion\nL148: \nL149: Finding the right health information online can feel like navigating a maze. While AI has the potential to be a powerful guide, our research shows that its success hinges on its ability to move beyond being a passive question-answerer and become an active conversational partner.\nL150: By designing our Wayfinding AI to be personal and proactive, we demonstrated how asking targeted questions in a well-structured interface can power an experience that users prefer over a more classical, question-answering experience, and thus enable people to obtain more helpful, relevant, and tailored information.\nL151: The results from our user studies provide strong evidence that this human-centered, conversational approach is a promising direction for the future of AI in health, helping people navigate their health journeys.\nL152: ## Acknowledgements\nL153: The research described here is joint work across Google Research, Google Health, and partnering teams.\nL154: We would like to thank Yuexing Hao, Abbi Ward, Amy Wang, Beverly Freeman, Serena Zhan, Diego Ardila, Jimmy Li, I-Ching Lee, Anna Iurchenko, Siyi Kou, Kartikeya Badola, Jimmy Hu, Bhawesh Kumar, Keith Johnson, Supriya Vijay, Justin Krogue, Avinatan Hassidim, Yossi Matias, Dale Webster, Sunny Virmani, Yun Liu, Quang Duong, Fereshteh Mahvar, Laura Vardoulakis, Tiffany Guo, and Meredith Ringel Morris for contributing or reviewing this work.\nL155: We would also like to thank the participants who contributed to these studies.\nL156:   * Labels:\nL157:   * cite43†Generative AI L158: \nL159:   * cite44†Health & Bioscience L160: \nL161:   * cite45†Human-Computer Interaction and Visualization L162: \nL163: × ❮ ❯\nL164: \nL165: cite46†Image: Wayfinder2_StudyDesign†storage.googleapis.com L166: \nL167: cite36†Image: Wayfinder4_HeroSankey†storage.googleapis.com L168: \nL169: cite47†Image: Wayfinder3_Results†storage.googleapis.com L170: \nL171: ×\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMore ways to work with your team and tools in ChatGPT | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/more-ways-to-work-with-your-team)\nciteturn3177view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://openai.com/index/more-ways-to-work-with-your-team\",\"lineno\":null}); Redirected to URL: https://openai.com/index/more-ways-to-work-with-your-team/; Total lines: 205\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: September 25, 2025\nL14: \nL15: cite3†Product L16: # More ways to work with your team and tools in ChatGPT\nL17: \nL18: New shared projects, smarter connectors, and compliance and security updates help teams get more done.\nL19: \nL20: Shared projects help teams get more done together\nL21:   * cite4†Shared projects help teams get more done together L22:     * cite5†Ways to use shared projects L23:     * cite6†How it works L24:   * cite7†Smarter answers from your team’s tools L25:     * cite8†Ways to use connectors L26:     * cite9†Recent updates L27:   * cite10†New compliance programs and admin controls keep company data secure L28:     * cite11†Recent updates L29:   * cite12†Looking ahead L30:   * cite4†Shared projects help teams get more done together L31:     * cite5†Ways to use shared projects L32:     * cite6†How it works L33:   * cite7†Smarter answers from your team’s tools L34:     * cite8†Ways to use connectors L35:     * cite9†Recent updates L36:   * cite10†New compliance programs and admin controls keep company data secure L37:     * cite11†Recent updates L38:   * cite12†Looking ahead L39: We want to help teams and organizations do their best work with ChatGPT. Today we’re rolling out shared projects for cite13†ChatGPT business⁠(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com plans; we’ve also recently added new connectors to team tools and improved response speed and accuracy, along with new compliance and admin features:\nL40:   * Shared projects help teams move faster: Teams can now work with ChatGPT toward a goal—whether supporting a customer account, creating content, or running monthly reports—while staying on the same page and keeping work consistent. Members can add files and instructions to a shared project together, and ChatGPT uses that context to tailor its responses so every new chat starts with the latest information.\nL41:   * More helpful responses from your team’s tools: With new connectors, ChatGPT can pull information from Gmail, Google Calendar, Microsoft Outlook, Microsoft Teams, SharePoint, GitHub, Dropbox, and Box for more relevant answers based on your work. Responses are now faster and more accurate, and ChatGPT can decide when to use the right connector for each prompt.\nL42:   * Compliance and admin features keep work data secure: New ISO 27001, 27017, 27018, and 27701 certifications, an expanded SOC 2 report, role-based access controls, and enhanced SSO help organizations confidently enable every employee with AI.\nL43: ## Shared projects help teams get more done together\nL44: Last year we launched projects to keep related chats, files, and instructions in one place so you can pick up work where you left off without re-explaining context to ChatGPT. Now you can share projects with teammates, adding files and instructions together to guide ChatGPT responses toward a shared goal. Members can chat with the project’s context to stay on the same page as new information gets added and create work that stays consistent in tone and style.\nL45: ### Ways to use shared projects\nL46: \nL47: In early testing, teams used them to coordinate:\nL48: \nL49:   * Client work: Keep all notes, proposals, and contracts in one place so account teams can draft follow-ups faster and stay aligned. For example, as an account manager, you can upload call notes and your solutions engineer can ask ChatGPT to summarize the latest updates to prepare for a customer demo.\nL50:   * Content creation: Use project-wide instructions to maintain a consistent voice and style. You can, for instance, tell ChatGPT to follow your brand guidelines so project members can create on-brand assets, from blogs to social copy, for an upcoming product launch.\nL51:   * Reporting: Split tasks while staying coordinated with shared instructions and uploaded datasets. Teams preparing monthly reporting can prepare updates for different audiences, such as an executive summary or a more detailed breakdown for finance.\nL52: ### How it works\nL53: \nL54: Project creators can invite teammates by email or link with two levels of access, chat or edit. Chat access lets members see and interact with the project’s chats, files, and instructions; edit access adds the ability to update instructions, upload or remove files, and invite others.\nL55: Shared projects have their own private memory, which lets ChatGPT remember details members share so you can start new conversations without explaining everything again. This is especially useful for long-running work with multiple follow-ups, and keeps sensitive data or client details safely within the project.\nL56: \nL57: Learn more in our cite14†Help Center.⁠(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com L58: What’s next: In this first version, project members can work asynchronously, with the ability to build on each other’s updates, start their own one-on-one conversations with ChatGPT, or branch another member’s conversation to explore a new idea. This release is an early step toward team collaboration in ChatGPT, and we’ll use your feedback to shape what comes next.\nL59: Availability: Shared projects are available to Business, Enterprise, and Edu plans today, with Free, Go, Plus, and Pro coming soon. They are defaulted off for Enterprise and Edu customers, and admins can control access. Projects can only be shared with workspace members for ChatGPT business plans.\nL60: ## Smarter answers from your team’s tools\nL61: Teams generate large amounts of documents, chats, and emails, so finding the right information when you need it can be hard. With connectors, ChatGPT pulls in relevant context from tools your team uses every day, like Google Drive, SharePoint, GitHub, Microsoft Teams, or Gmail, so you can onboard to a new codebase, write a marketing brief using the latest template, or summarize important emails. It’s like working with a teammate who gets your work and can give expert answers on almost any topic.\nL62: Recent updates make connectors even more useful: responses are faster and more accurate and ChatGPT now knows when to use connectors automatically. New email and calendar connectors let you pull context from upcoming meetings and recent conversations so you can draft agendas, follow up on action items, or quickly find details without switching apps.\nL63: ### Ways to use connectors\nL64: \nL65: Gmail GitHub Google Drive\nL66: \nL67: How teams use connectors today:\nL68: \nL69:   * Communication and writing: Draft updates, synthesize documents, or follow guidelines and templates (e.g., “Using our web template in Google Drive, create a brief for [x]”).\nL70: \nL71:   * Meetings and collaboration: Prepare for meetings, create agendas, and identify action items (e.g., “Review my latest emails with [customer/company] to prep for our next call”).\nL72:   * Organization and productivity: Quickly find important documents or answers in company data (e.g., “How do I submit an expense report?”).\nL73: \nL74:   * Analysis and reporting: Analyze data for market research or performance tracking (e.g., “Tell me which campaigns drove our highest-value deals in HubSpot”).\nL75: \nL76:   * Coding and technical tasks: Understand codebases, review pull requests, or write documentation (e.g., “How is [feature] implemented in our codebase?”).\nL77: cite15†Explore more starter prompts for connectors. ⁠(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com L78: \nL79: LogicMonitor Endava Plex Coffee\nL80: ### Recent updates\nL81: \nL82: Added more productivity tools: New connectors to Gmail, Google Calendar, Google Contacts, Microsoft Outlook and Calendar, and Microsoft Teams give ChatGPT a more complete view of projects, communications, and schedules, so teams spend less time chasing information across tools.\nL83: ChatGPT knows when to use connectors: We’ve improved ChatGPT’s ability to automatically decide when to use connectors, search the web, or rely on its own training—making your company context a more seamless part of the conversation. It can also pick the most relevant connector based on the question, so you no longer need to turn individual connectors on in every new chat.\nL84: Faster, more accurate responses: ChatGPT can now sync data from GitHub, SharePoint, Dropbox, and Box ahead of time, so it responds faster and more accurately. Learn more about cite16†synced connectors⁠(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com .\nL85: What’s next: In the coming weeks, we’ll roll out new connectors for popular team tools across CRM, communication, knowledge stores, and ticketing, bringing ChatGPT closer to everything your business runs on. Soon, we’ll also make connectors available in projects and GPTs.\nL86: Availability: Connectors are now generally available and can be enabled in cite17†Settings⁠(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com . Admins control user access for ChatGPT business plans, and they’re off by default for Enterprise and Edu. As always, connectors respect existing content permissions, so ChatGPT only sees what each person is allowed to access, and all data remains encrypted in transit and at rest.\nL87: We never train on content shared with ChatGPT for our business plans by defaultcite18†⁠⁠(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com . See our cite19†Help Center⁠(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com for details on availability by plan.\nL88: ## New compliance programs and admin controls keep company data secure\nL89: \nL90: As we make ChatGPT more valuable for the workplace, we’re equally focused on helping organizations deploy it across their company responsibly and with confidence. To support this, we’ve added new compliance and admin features that give companies more control over who can access what, where their data lives, and how it’s protected.\nL91: ### Recent updates\nL92: \nL93: Compliance certifications: OpenAI’s Information Security Management System is now certified by Schellman to ISO/IEC 27001, 27017, 27018, and 27701 standards. We’ve also expanded our SOC 2 compliance to cover four Trust Services Criteria, now including Security, Confidentiality, Availability and Privacy. Customers can view our cite20†ISO certificate⁠(opens in a new window)†trust.openai.com and cite21†SOC 2 report⁠(opens in a new window)†trust.openai.com on our Trust Portal.\nL94: Role-based access controls: ChatGPT Enterprise and Edu admins can cite22†create custom roles and set group-level permissions⁠(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com for tools like connectors, projects, GPTs, search, and more. This helps align access with company policies to streamline management as workspaces grow.\nL95: Expanded SSO support: We rolled out cite23†OIDC support⁠(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com for ChatGPT Business, Enterprise, Edu, and API customers, in addition to SAML, giving customers more flexibility to integrate with their existing identity providers.\nL96: IP allowlisting: Admins can now enable cite24†IP allowlisting⁠(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com as an optional security feature for ChatGPT Enterprise and Edu workspaces. When turned on, only requests from the IP addresses you specify will be allowed. Any request from an unapproved IP is automatically blocked, even if the user has valid credentials.\nL97: \nL98: These updates build on our existing, robust enterprise-grade security, data privacy, and compliance programs. cite25†Learn more⁠ .\nL99: ## Looking ahead\nL100: \nL101: OpenAI now serves over 5 million ChatGPT business users as companies move from experimentation to daily, mission-critical use. To support this shift, we’ll continue making ChatGPT a secure, future-proof platform that gets better as AI advances to help teams work smarter every day.\nL102: \nL103: Learn more about cite13†ChatGPT Business⁠(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com or cite26†contact our team⁠(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com for more information on OpenAI’s products.\nL104:   * cite27†2025 L105:   * cite28†ChatGPT L106: ## Author\nL107: \nL108: OpenAI\nL109: ## Keep reading\nL110: \nL111: cite29†View all L112: \nL113: cite30†Building advertising for the way people use AI ProductOct 5, 2026 L114: \nL115: cite31†A practical guide to building with GPT-6 ProductOct 2, 2026 L116: \nL117: cite32†DevDay 2026 Recap CompanySep 29, 2026 L118: \nL119: Research\nL120: \nL121:   * cite33†Research Index L122:   * cite34†Research Overview L123:   * cite35†Economic Research L124: \nL125: Latest Advancements\nL126: \nL127:   * cite36†GPT-6.1 Sol L128:   * cite37†GPT-6 Astra L129:   * cite38†GPT-5.6 L130:   * cite39†GPT-5.5 L131: \nL132: Safety\nL133:   * cite40†Safety Approach L134:   * cite41†Deployment Safety(opens in a new window)†deploymentsafety.openai.com L135:   * cite42†Security & Privacy L136:   * cite43†Trust & Transparency L137: \nL138: Products\nL139:   * cite2†ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L140:   * cite44†ChatGPT Business(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L141:   * cite45†ChatGPT Enterprise(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L142:   * cite46†ChatGPT for Education(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L143:   * cite47†Codex†chatgpt.com L144:   * cite48†Dots(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L145:   * cite49†Release Notes L146: \nL147: API Platform\nL148: \nL149:   * cite50†Overview L150:   * cite51†API Log In(opens in a new window)†platform.openai.com L151:   * cite52†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L152: \nL153: Business\